  "id": "haqiba_sudanese",
  "name": "Haqiba (Omdurman vocal song, 1920s-1940s)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Urban vocal song of Omdurman that took shape in the 1920s, when singers carried the style of Sufi madih praise-singing over to secular love poetry in colloquial Sudanese Arabic, accompanied only by a responding chorus, the tambourine and hand-clapping. Pioneers included Mohamed Ahmed Sorour, Abdel Karim Karouma and the poet-singer Khalil Farah, many of whose songs were issued on 78 rpm discs; the name comes from the later radio programme Haqibat al-Fann ('the bag of art') that rebroadcast them.",
  "recipe": "classic haqiba, ballad Poetry, med east poetic branch. riq, hand clap. parlor. large-diaphragm condenser, tube pre, German tube console. 78rpm shellac.",
